.TH MMIXDB 1 "2026-08-15" "checksmix 0.3.6"
.SH NAME
mmixdb \- interactive source-level debugger for MMIX assembly sources
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mmixdb
.RB [ \-\-fullname ]
.I FILE.mms
.RI [ FILE.mms ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mmixdb
is an interactive source-level debugger for MMIX assembly
.RB ( .mms )
sources. It assembles one or more source files and presents a command loop
for stepping through a program and inspecting its state.
.PP
Only
.B .mms
sources are accepted; a
.B .mmo
object file is rejected with an explicit error, because source-line
debugging requires the source. Multiple
.B .mms
files are assembled into one shared symbol space before execution, exactly
as
.B checksmix run
does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-fullname , \ \-\-emacs
Emit Emacs GUD stop markers on every stop, for use as the debugger under
.B M-x mmixdb
or equivalent. Automatically enabled when the environment variable
.B INSIDE_EMACS
is set, so an Emacs session need not pass the flag explicitly. The marker
has gdb\(aqs
.B \-\-fullname
shape, so Emacs\(aq own gdb marker filter parses it unchanged.
.PP
.I contrib/mmixdb.el
provides
.BR "M-x mmixdb" .
Put
.I contrib
on
.B load-path
and require it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
(add-to-list \(aqload-path "/path/to/checksmix/contrib")
(require \(aqmmixdb)
.fi
.RE
.PP
or autoload it instead:
.PP
.RS
.nf
(autoload \(aqmmixdb "mmixdb" "Run mmixdb under gud-mode." t)
.fi
.RE
.SH COMMANDS
At the
.B (mmixdb)
prompt, most commands accept both a short letter and a long word; Emacs GUD
sends the long words. Blank input repeats the last command.
.TP
.BR s ", " step
Execute one source line, following into calls and branches. Stops at the
first address on a different line, or at a breakpoint or a halt reached
first. Also stops, distinguishably, if an internal step budget is exhausted
first.
.TP
.BR n ", " next
Execute one source line, stepping over any call it makes: the new line
counts only once the call depth is back at or below where it started. Also
stops, distinguishably, if an internal step budget is exhausted first.
.TP
.BR si ", " stepi
Execute exactly one instruction, following into calls and branches. Inside a
multi-tetra expansion the stop names the enclosing source line with the
address in front.
.TP
.BR c ", " continue
Resume, single-stepping until a subsequent breakpoint or a halt. A
breakpoint at the current address does not fire; the instruction there runs
first. Also stops, distinguishably, if an internal step budget is exhausted
first.
.TP
.BR r ", " run
Reset to the freshly-loaded image, then run on to a breakpoint or a halt.
Unlike
.BR continue ,
a breakpoint on the entry point fires: the reset means nothing has executed
yet.
.B run
is the one resume command that works after the program has exited.
.TP
.BR "b " \fILINE\fR|\fILABEL\fR ", " "break " \fILINE\fR|\fILABEL\fR
Set a breakpoint at a decimal source line number in the current file, or
at an exact label. No other address form is accepted.
.TP
.BR "d " \fILINE\fR|\fILABEL\fR ", " "delete " \fILINE\fR|\fILABEL\fR ", " d ", " delete
Delete the breakpoint at a decimal source line number or an exact label,
resolved exactly as
.BR break .
With no argument, delete every breakpoint.
.TP
.BR "p " \fIARG\fR ", " "print " \fIARG\fR
Print a general register (\fB$\fIN\fR or bare \fIN\fR), a special
register, a label's address, an IS/GREG symbol or the memory octa at the
hex address's aligned 8-byte base (MMIX has no unaligned access).
.TP
.BR bt ", " backtrace ", " "info reg" ", " "info registers"
Print the full register dump.
.TP
.BR "info break" ", " "info breakpoints"
List every currently-set breakpoint with its resolved source location, or
report that none are set.
.TP
.BR l ", " list
Print source lines around the current program counter.
.TP
.BR h ", " help ", " ?
Show the command summary.
.TP
.BR q ", " quit ", " exit
Exit the debugger.
.PP
Once the program has exited,
.BR step ,
.BR stepi ,
.B next
and
.B continue
are refused with
.RB \(lq "The program is not being run." \(rq;
.B run
restarts it.
.BR print ,
.BR state ,
.BR list ,
.B delete
and
.B "info break"
keep answering, since the register file is what the program was run to
show.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Enable tracing output from the emulator. Example:
.BR RUST_LOG=checksmix=debug .
.TP
.B INSIDE_EMACS
When set, auto-enables
.B \-\-fullname
stop markers, as Emacs sets this variable for subprocesses of a
.B compile
or
.B gud
session.
.SH EXAMPLES
Debug a single source file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mmixdb examples/hello_world.mms
.fi
.RE
.PP
Debug multiple sources assembled into one shared symbol space:
.PP
.RS
.nf
mmixdb examples/module1.mms examples/module2.mms
.fi
.RE
.PP
Set a breakpoint at a label, then run to it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
(mmixdb) break Main
(mmixdb) run
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR checksmix (1),
.BR mmixasm (1)
